  "comment": "SIXTEENTH PACK. Thai: a script that writes words TOGETHER but sentences APART — the third case between Chinese (no spaces) and Latin (spaces between words), and the `longest-match` declaration turns out to cover it, because the segmenter SKIPS a space rather than requiring one. Vowels written BEFORE the consonant they follow in sound and tone marks written ABOVE it: `graphemes` is a flat set and can say nothing of either. NO plural and NO person — classifiers ride in the noun's own paradigm, six verb cells hold one form. The forge hard-codes a space and a Latin full stop into two kinds; Thai writes neither. TIER 1.",
